# RP-1.3: Data-oriented design & SoA layout

## Abstract
This paper explores Data-Oriented Design and Struct-of-Arrays memory layouts in the openOODA language. The system provides first-class support for columnar layouts to guarantee cache locality. It also includes native zero-copy serialization. This capability allows the system to parse network packets without redundant processing cost.

## 1. Introduction
Modern processors stall when they wait for memory. Performance requires strict attention to hardware reality. This reality includes cache lines, prefetching, and memory layouts. Traditional object-oriented design groups diverse fields together. This approach pulls cold fields into the cache and decreases memory bandwidth.

Data-Oriented Design solves these performance problems. It transforms large homogeneous datasets using contiguous fields. It ensures predictable memory access patterns. Without first-class language support, programmers must manage memory layouts manually. This manual process causes agents and humans to regress to inefficient data structures. Refactoring to efficient layouts becomes invasive and lacks type safety.

## 2. Architecture
The openOODA language implements first-class layout declarations. Programmers declare columnar layouts directly in the type system. A record type can specify its storage strategy. The compiler understands that the lengths of field vectors remain coupled.

This architecture enables zero-copy serialization. Validated buffers operate as typed structures. The system does not copy data for each field during deserialization. Zero processing cost means the system performs no redundant copies or transformations. The system still performs validation, checksums, and capability checks.

The design integrates with the capability system. When the system parses untrusted network bytes, it requires explicit buffer capabilities. The zero-copy mechanism prevents type confusion while maintaining high throughput.

## 3. Methodology
The methodology applies Data-Oriented Design as a core language feature rather than an external framework. The compiler uses declarative layouts to guide automated agents. When agents generate code, they target these declared layouts. They do not invent undocumented memory patterns.

The system separates core data structures from operating system interactions. The standard library keeps pure table algorithms in the core module. The compiler intermediate representation itself uses columnar layouts. This choice improves the speed of the self-hosted compiler.

## 4. Conclusion
Data-Oriented Design provides critical performance guarantees for openOODA. First-class language support prevents systematic cache thrashing. It eliminates hidden copies at protocol boundaries. By integrating memory layout directly into the type system, the language enables both human developers and artificial intelligence agents to write highly efficient software.

